SOAPBOX: Time flies when you’re having fun!
SOAPBOX: CQ WW CW 2025 was my 10th contest from Greenland and without a doubt the
SOAPBOX: roughest.
SOAPBOX: There really is no mercy for the wicked up here. I started off by arriving in
SOAPBOX: Kangerlussuaq on Wednesday after two days of work in Nuuk. I then immediately
SOAPBOX: came down with a fever 12 hours after.
SOAPBOX: I was miserable and felt weak. The QTH I'm using has the basics but there's
SOAPBOX: always a lot of work to do to turn it into a functioning contest station. Adding
SOAPBOX: to this was that the 160/80m antenna turned out to be broken and I had tons of
SOAPBOX: other small problems to fix. I was running out of time.
SOAPBOX: Friday came and I felt better. I managed to make an improvised vertical for 160
SOAPBOX: and a dipole for 80. I worked very hard all day and the station got ready only
SOAPBOX: 2.5 hours before the contest. Needless to say I was exhausted from the very
SOAPBOX: start.
SOAPBOX: The first couple of hours went okay. The K Index had been hovering around 4 for
SOAPBOX: a few days and lots of small solar flares kept making my life exceedingly
SOAPBOX: difficult. A few hours in, my rate started dropping. I was lucky to work a few
SOAPBOX: contacts on 160 and 80, but then propagation took a turn for the worse. Every
SOAPBOX: single signal started hissing.
SOAPBOX: I'd learned from previous years that doing the full 48 hours is counter
SOAPBOX: productive for most people and especially for me. A couple of naps were on the
SOAPBOX: menu from the start and I decided to take one. I slept for about two hours and
SOAPBOX: got back up only to find the bands were in an even worse shape. All day I was
SOAPBOX: fighting for QSOs. I had managed about 1300 when sunset rolled around on
SOAPBOX: Saturday. 1300 hissing, fluttering, screeching QSOs.
SOAPBOX: My pileups became huge as people started to realise I was there and they needed
SOAPBOX: me for perhaps a double mult. That's normal and is in part the reason I go up
SOAPBOX: here. A big pileup is great. I love that stuff - the bigger the better! But, a
SOAPBOX: pileup of 100 stations all with crazy flutter is indistinguishable from torture.
SOAPBOX: During a contest there is no waiting. People just call and you better keep up or
SOAPBOX: they start panicking and all matters of decency and discipline go out the
SOAPBOX: window. This was too much. I lost it. My mind.
SOAPBOX: This contest broke me. I had overworked myself and now I had to pay the price. I
SOAPBOX: had to throw in the towel as hallucinations had set in and my head was spinning.
SOAPBOX: So I slept again. For six hours. I got up with the Sun on the second day, but
SOAPBOX: everyone was still hissing at me. Usually you say that at the 24 hour mark you
SOAPBOX: can estimate your final score by doubling your points. I was at around a measly
SOAPBOX: million at this point so I needed nothing short of a miracle – or go home in
SOAPBOX: shame with the worst result in over 10 years.
SOAPBOX: Then it happened. 10 meters saved my sanity as it opened because it is much less
SOAPBOX: susceptible to the flutter. Slowly rates climbed and I finally got above 200
SOAPBOX: QSOs per hour in bursts. Around midday the K Index dropped to 3 and then to 2.
SOAPBOX: It was showtime!
SOAPBOX: I started picking up the pieces. My pileups became so big that they were almost
SOAPBOX: impossible to manage and my rate dropped to 150-170 QSOs per hour – this time
SOAPBOX: because of the sheer number of stations calling. I kept at it - trying to work
SOAPBOX: mults on the second radio at the same time with limited success.
SOAPBOX: I went into this thinking I'd do some 2BSIQ but at this point in the contest
SOAPBOX: it was out of the question. One pileup was more than enough to handle. OX is a
SOAPBOX: special place. Being on top of the world you have unique propagation conditions
SOAPBOX: where all corners of the world may come in at the same time. The biggest pileups
SOAPBOX: I've ever had have been in CQ WW in Greenland by far. I imagine it's easier
SOAPBOX: if you're at a less rare DXCC. I of course also just need to get much better
SOAPBOX: at what I do. This time wasn't it.
SOAPBOX: During the final ten hours of the contest I more than tripled my mult count. 10
SOAPBOX: and 15 showed their pretty side at night with ZL7, KH6, and VK/ZL getting into
SOAPBOX: my log. I pretty much kept my rate going while working mults on the side. It was
SOAPBOX: wonderful!
SOAPBOX: The contest ended on a high note and I've never been this thoroughly tested
SOAPBOX: before. So why the heck do I keep doing this? Because, when it works it is
SOAPBOX: absolutely magical! It's just a shame it's such a rare treat.
SOAPBOX: Thanks to everyone who called and answered! 73 es CU all next year,
SOAPBOX: - Nadi
